<p>In studying the relationship between productivity and efficiency in 1913, French agricultural engineer Maximilien Ringelmann found that members of a group perform worse on average as the size of the group increases. In a tug of war, as the size of a team grows, each member begins to &#8220;loaf,&#8221; relying on his teammates to put forth the necessary effort. Worse, the team&#8217;s growing size makes it increasingly difficult to coordinate its actions.</p>
<p>Interestingly, though, ants have been shown to increase in efficiency when working in teams. New World army ants can carry larger loads as a team than if the burden were distributed among them individually, and Weaver ants organize pulling chains that can effectively double the strength of each member. So &#8220;the Ringelmann effect&#8221; isn&#8217;t inescapable.</p>
